{"answer":"There are 7 subsets. Partition any nonempty subset by its unique largest element m. Since the total is 9, m is in {4,5,6,7,8}: m<=3 is impossible because the total of all elements at most 3 is 1+2+3=6. The residual sums are:\n\n- m=8: residual 1 gives {1,8}.\n- m=7: residual 2 gives {2,7}.\n- m=6: residual 3 gives {3,6} and {1,2,6}.\n- m=5: residual 4 gives {4,5} and {1,3,5}.\n- m=4: residual 5 from {1,2,3} gives {2,3,4}.\n\nThus the seven subsets are {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {1,2,6}, {4,5}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}. For example, {1,8} has sum 9. The cases are exhaustive because every candidate has exactly one largest element, and they are disjoint because distinct largest elements cannot describe the same subset.","approaches":[{"description":"Classify each subset by its unique largest element, then enumerate the smaller-element subsets that achieve the corresponding residual sum.","id":"largest-element-cases","outcome":"candidate","reason":"This supplies both the enumeration and a disjoint, complete exhaustiveness partition."}],"change_log":[],"claims":[{"basis":"question_premise","basis_reference":"The question specifies subsets of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8} with sum 9.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":[],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"problem-data","kind":"definition","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Let S={1,2,3,4,5,6,7,8}; the required subset sum is 9.","step_ids":[],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived by the largest-element partition in steps largest-bound through count-total.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["problem-data"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"enumeration","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"Exactly seven subsets of S have sum 9: {1,8}, {2,7}, {3,6}, {1,2,6}, {4,5}, {1,3,5}, and {2,3,4}.","step_ids":["largest-bound","case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four","count-total"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"The listed set is one member of the enumeration and its elements sum to 9.","citations":[],"critical":false,"depends_on":["enumeration"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"example-subset","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"{1,8} is a requested example subset, since 1+8=9.","step_ids":["case-eight"],"tool_ids":[]},{"basis":"derivation","basis_reference":"Derived from uniqueness of a subset's largest element and the complete residual-sum cases.","citations":[],"critical":true,"depends_on":["enumeration"],"discharged_by_step_ids":[],"id":"exhaustiveness","kind":"deduction","scope_step_ids":[],"statement":"The enumeration is exhaustive and has no overlap between cases.","step_ids":["partition-disjoint","count-total"],"tool_ids":[]}],"open_questions":[],"proof_steps":[{"citations":[],"depends_on":[],"id":"largest-bound","justification":"If the largest element were at most 3, the subset sum would be at most 1+2+3=6, not 9. A largest element cannot exceed 8 because it belongs to S. Hence it is one of 4,5,6,7,8.","statement":"Every subset of S with sum 9 has largest element m in {4,5,6,7,8}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-bound"],"id":"case-eight","justification":"With largest element 8, the smaller elements must sum to 9-8=1. The only subset of {1,2,3,4,5,6,7} with sum 1 is {1}.","statement":"The m=8 case contributes exactly {1,8}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-bound"],"id":"case-seven","justification":"With largest element 7, the smaller elements must sum to 2. The only subset of {1,2,3,4,5,6} with sum 2 is {2}.","statement":"The m=7 case contributes exactly {2,7}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-bound"],"id":"case-six","justification":"With largest element 6, the smaller elements must sum to 3. From {1,2,3,4,5}, the only distinct-element subsets with sum 3 are {3} and {1,2}.","statement":"The m=6 case contributes exactly {3,6} and {1,2,6}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-bound"],"id":"case-five","justification":"With largest element 5, the smaller elements must sum to 4. From {1,2,3,4}, the only distinct-element subsets with sum 4 are {4} and {1,3}.","statement":"The m=5 case contributes exactly {4,5} and {1,3,5}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["largest-bound"],"id":"case-four","justification":"With largest element 4, the smaller elements must sum to 5. Among subsets of {1,2,3}, only {2,3} sums to 5.","statement":"The m=4 case contributes exactly {2,3,4}."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four"],"id":"partition-disjoint","justification":"Every nonempty finite subset has one unique largest element. Therefore the five largest-element cases cover every eligible subset and cannot overlap.","statement":"The five cases form a complete, mutually disjoint partition of all eligible subsets."},{"citations":[],"depends_on":["case-eight","case-seven","case-six","case-five","case-four","partition-disjoint"],"id":"count-total","justification":"The respective case counts are 1, 1, 2, 2, and 1. Their sum is 7; disjointness prevents double-counting, and completeness prevents omissions.","statement":"The total number of eligible subsets is 1+1+2+2+1=7."}],"question_status":"answered","tool_requests":[]}
